     "result_text_tail": "six-month check, because one quarter can't show whether churn settles.\n- The deck now flags that mid-market churn also rose (0.6% to 0.8%). The answer is most sensitive to how mid-market customers react at renewal: the 10% step stops paying if their monthly churn passes about 1.6%.\n\n**Choices I made for you**\n- Audience: the CEO and leadership team approving the plan in December.\n- No separate approval round for the storyline.\n- Five sections merged into four so the executive summary fits on one page at full size.\n- The 10%, 8% and 7% steps each come from evidence in the pack.\n- I set a stop level for each guardrail measure on the last plan slide; you may want to change them.\n\n**Limits of the numbers**\n- There are no \"?\" placeholders. Every number comes from the briefing pack or a formula over it.\n- The 2027 ARR figures and the onboarding split are our estimates. Their basis is shown on the slides and their assumptions in the workbook.\n- The pack has no figure for new-customer ARR, so the ARR outlook covers existing customers only.\n- The pack doesn't say whether multi-year enterprise contracts allow a price escalator before renewal. If they do, that is up to about $1.0M more; it's footnoted and is one of the next steps.\n- The board dashboard's \"1.4% \u2192 2.3%\" churn figure doesn't match the segment data either way it could be weighted. A backup slide shows the mismatch.\n\nNext, I could switch the theme, or add new-customer ARR to the 2027 outlook if you can get a bookings figure."
